WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.867
Message queues.

2
00:00:02.167 --> 00:00:06.085
A queue lets your system survive a sudden spike without falling over.

3
00:00:06.085 --> 00:00:06.767
Here is how.

4
00:00:07.967 --> 00:00:12.833
A message queue decouples the code that produces work from the code that consumes it.

5
00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:16.800
The producer drops a job on the queue and returns immediately.

6
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.433
Consumers pull jobs at whatever rate they can sustain.

7
00:00:21.633 --> 00:00:23.633
Here is the shock absorber at work.

8
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:26.700
A burst of four hundred jobs per second arrives.

9
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.975
But the workers drain at a steady fifty per second.

10
00:00:29.975 --> 00:00:31.900
The extra jobs wait in the queue.

11
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.377
Workers never see the spike, so they never fall over.

12
00:00:35.377 --> 00:00:38.733
And if a worker crashes, its job goes back on the queue.

13
00:00:39.933 --> 00:00:43.100
Now the fine print that separates seniors from juniors.

14
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.267
The standard guarantee is at-least-once delivery.

15
00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:53.000
Exactly-once across processes is practically a myth, so the same job will occasionally arrive twice.

16
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:59.067
Making the consumer idempotent, so a duplicate changes nothing, is your job, not the queue's.

17
00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:02.167
Here is a worker that handles it.

18
00:01:02.467 --> 00:01:04.300
It blocks until a job arrives.

19
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.496
Then it claims the job with set if not exists.

20
00:01:07.496 --> 00:01:10.833
If the claim fails, it is a duplicate, so we skip it.

21
00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:15.367
If the work fails, we release the claim and push the job back for a retry.

22
00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:21.367
A big solar storm hits, and NASA publishes three hundred new reports in an hour.

23
00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:26.208
Instead of embedding inline, ingest drops three hundred jobs on the queue.

24
00:01:26.208 --> 00:01:29.400
Four workers drain them at the embedding rate limit.

25
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.386
Now kill a worker mid job.

26
00:01:31.386 --> 00:01:37.419
Another worker gets the job, and the claim key makes sure the chunk is embedded exactly once.

27
00:01:37.419 --> 00:01:39.300
That kill test is your proof.

28
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.277
Let's recap.

29
00:01:41.277 --> 00:01:43.933
Queues decouple producers from consumers.

30
00:01:44.233 --> 00:01:46.433
They absorb spikes and retry failures.

31
00:01:46.733 --> 00:01:48.433
Delivery is at least once.

32
00:01:48.733 --> 00:01:51.091
So make your consumers idempotent.

33
00:01:51.091 --> 00:01:52.200
Next up: Celery.
